                'blocks'  => [
                    [ 'p', 'Until recently, laser welding was reserved for large factories. Today compact machines with a fibre source are moving into metalworking shops, metal furniture studios and companies building custom structures. Here is how the technology works and when it makes sense.' ],
                    [ 'h2', 'How does a laser welder work?' ],
                    [ 'p', 'A fibre source produces a beam with a high power density, which the head focuses on a small spot. The metal melts very locally, so heat does not spread widely through the part. The result is a narrow, even weld and less risk of distorting thin sheet metal.' ],
                    [ 'h2', 'Laser versus MIG and TIG' ],
                    [ 'ul', [
                        'Speed: on thin and medium sheet metal, laser welding is usually considerably faster than TIG.',
                        'Appearance: the narrow weld often needs less grinding and polishing.',
                        'Less distortion: the small heat-affected zone reduces warping.',
                        'Shorter learning curve: an even weld is easier to achieve than with TIG, which takes a lot of practice.',
